  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms through early \nevening, with a few more scattered showers overnight. A few storms \nmay produce isolated strong wind gusts and brief heavy downpours.\n\nA sharp mid level trough will advance from Ontario to Quebec \ntonight, with DPVA and height falls spreading east across the \neastern Great Lakes through this evening and providing large scale \nascent. The associated surface cold front will come through in \npieces, with the first boundary crossing the eastern Great Lakes \nearly this evening, and a secondary cold front overnight. \n\nConvective initiation will primarily occur on lake breeze \nconvergence zones this afternoon, with the main boundary stretching \nfrom the Niagara Peninsula east to Rochester. Some additional \ndevelopment will also occur along the southern edge of enhanced flow \noff Lake Erie from the western Southern Tier into the Finger Lakes. \nThe first cold front and associated veering low/mid level flow will \ncapture these lake breeze initiated storms and move them southeast \nacross the area late this afternoon through early evening. A few \nscattered showers may linger through the evening hours across the \nhigher terrain of the Finger Lakes and Tug Hill region, but any \nthunderstorms should end by around or shortly after sunset.\n\nStrong shear profiles are in place across the eastern Great Lakes as \na belt of enhanced flow propagates through the base of the digging \ntrough. Weak to moderate instability will be in place, although \nrelatively poor mid level lapse rates will yield tall/skinny CAPE \nprofiles. Given the strong shear profiles, any larger sustained \nupdrafts will have the potential to produce isolated damaging wind \ngusts and hail, but latest CAMS guidance suggests this potential \nwill remain limited in coverage and scope. The main window for \nstronger storms will be roughly 2 PM to 7 PM.\n\nOvernight, the secondary cold front will move southeast across the \neastern Great Lakes. The airmass aloft may grow cold enough to \nsupport a brief period of scattered light lake effect rain showers, \nmainly across the Genesee Valley and western Finger Lakes. Any \nshowers that develop will end early Monday morning as a much drier \nsynoptic scale airmass arrives.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Brief cooldown Monday through early Tuesday morning, \nthen trending warmer and unsettled through the middle of the week.\n\nA much cooler airmass will briefly settle into the eastern Great \nLakes Monday, with 850MB temps bottoming out in the +4C to +6C \nrange. This will only support highs in the mid to upper 60s for \nlower elevations, and lower 60s for higher terrain. The cool \nnorthwest flow will support some lake effect and upslope clouds in \nthe morning, but these will scatter out later in the day as dry air \nand subsidence build into the region.\n\nSurface high pressure will settle over NY and western New England \nMonday night, with good radiational cooling conditions allowing for \nlows to drop back into the 40s areawide. A few of the normally \ncolder Southern Tier Valleys and Tug Hill region may make a run at \nthe upper 30s, but any patchy frost is likely to remain confined to \nthe coldest spots in the Adirondacks.\n\nThe high will move quickly east off the New England coast Tuesday, \nwith strong warm advection developing following a chilly start to \nthe day.\n\nA cold front will cross the Great Lakes Tuesday night through \nWednesday, bringing the next chance of showers. The front may stall \nand oscillate back and forth across the Ohio Valley and eastern \nGreat Lakes Wednesday night through Thursday night, supporting \nongoing chances of showers and a few thunderstorms.",
